WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.030 --> 00:00:05.359
Logan ranks among the very best superhero
movies of all time and although director James

00:00:05.359 --> 00:00:09.280
Mangold said that he got everything he wanted
in the final cut of the movie, there are still

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:13.059
some fascinating scenes that didn't make it
into the film.

00:00:13.059 --> 00:00:15.980
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here,
and with the Blu-ray of Logan out now, I'm

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:20.130
going to break down all the official deleted
scenes and discuss some easter eggs and details

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:22.670
we never got to see in the theatrical release.

00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:26.630
Quick spoiler warning: if you haven't seen
Logan yet, there will be spoilers for the

00:00:26.630 --> 00:00:27.630
movie.

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:31.230
If you want to avoid them, tap up here for
my spoiler-free review and come back after

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:32.230
you've seen the film!

00:00:32.230 --> 00:00:36.969
There are a couple of cut scenes involving
the mutant children that Logan and Laura meet

00:00:36.969 --> 00:00:37.969
at Eden.

00:00:37.969 --> 00:00:41.899
First up is a scene with Bobby, a young mutant
with electrical powers, who has an action

00:00:41.899 --> 00:00:44.690
figure of Sabretooth and another of Wolverine.

00:00:44.690 --> 00:00:47.679
Bobby asks Logan if Sabretooth was ever real.

00:00:47.679 --> 00:00:52.480
Logan explains to Bobby that some of it was
true and that Sabretooth was real and that

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:56.920
both he and Victor were developed as part
of a weapons program just like Bobby was.

00:00:56.920 --> 00:01:01.649
The scene is a nod back to X-Men Origins:
Wolverine where we saw Wolverine and Sabretooth's

00:01:01.649 --> 00:01:03.079
early history as [half-]brothers.

00:01:03.079 --> 00:01:08.770
The scene also has a bit of hidden meaning
as we know from Bobby's file that the DNA

00:01:08.770 --> 00:01:13.799
source for his electrical powers comes from
Christopher Bradley, aka Bolt, and it was

00:01:13.799 --> 00:01:16.159
Sabretooth who killed Chris Bradley in Origins:
Wolverine.

00:01:16.159 --> 00:01:20.439
There's also a tender moment when Bobby tries
to convince Logan that he's doing good in

00:01:20.439 --> 00:01:24.530
the world now despite any bad things he might
have done in the past and I feel that this

00:01:24.530 --> 00:01:28.750
deleted moment would have made the scene later
when Bobby is holding the Wolverine action

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:32.250
figure at Logan's funeral even more poignant.

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:36.840
The Sabretooth action figure is also interesting
because, during Logan's development, Liev

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:41.259
Schreiber was approached to return one final
time as Sabretooth in a potential storyline

00:01:41.259 --> 00:01:45.030
where Logan found his brother Victor in charge
of a major casino.

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:49.259
Many fans would have loved to have seen the
pair reunited as their performances in X-Men

00:01:49.259 --> 00:01:53.469
Origins: Wolverine are one of the few bright
spots in what is often considered one of the

00:01:53.469 --> 00:01:55.960
worst X-Men movies to date.

00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:59.860
Sadly, scheduling conflicts and the way the
story developed mean that his mention in a

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:03.280
deleted scene is the best nod we got to Sabretooth
in Logan.

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:08.039
Now, there's a pretty dark deleted scene involving
one of the other mutant kids who director

00:02:08.039 --> 00:02:10.679
James Mangold has called the "Puppet Master".

00:02:10.679 --> 00:02:15.680
This child uses his powers of mental manipulation
to stop a Reaver who's chasing him by making

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:18.450
him turn on and shoot his fellow Reavers.

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:23.739
And in an even darker twist, the kid then
makes that same Reaver turn the gun on himself.

00:02:23.739 --> 00:02:28.790
After Caliban blew himself up, there was a
deleted extended death scene where, although

00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:32.650
badly injured, he managed to get out of the
truck and crawl towards Logan.

00:02:32.650 --> 00:02:36.879
In that scene, Logan and Laura lock eyes with
Caliban before he's finally overcome by his

00:02:36.879 --> 00:02:38.819
injuries and they walk away.

00:02:38.819 --> 00:02:42.879
I think this extended scene was probably better
left out as it was fairly clear in the film

00:02:42.879 --> 00:02:47.520
that Caliban wasn't going to survive after
dropping a couple of grenades near himself.

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:51.330
And if the scene had been in the film, it
would have no doubt seemed odd or even somewhat

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:56.489
callous that Logan took Charles but not Caliban
with them, however, this cut scene does give

00:02:56.489 --> 00:03:01.290
a little closure to Caliban's story and maybe
highlights that his sacrifice wasn't in vain

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:04.750
and was acknowledged by both Logan and Laura.

00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:09.099
Actor Richard E. Grant felt rather underused
in Logan, however, there's a little extra

00:03:09.099 --> 00:03:13.410
deleted scene with Dr Rice where Pierce shows
him that's he uncovered some coordinates to

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:18.060
a location in the Badlands, a reservation
on the border that's also in an X-Men comic.

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:22.440
We then discover that Rice is holding hostage
a nurse who worked at Alkali Transigen and

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:25.909
demanding she explain the meaning of the comic
book and the coordinates.

00:03:25.909 --> 00:03:30.280
Although not totally necessary, the scene
does give a touch more screen time to Richard

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:34.750
E. Grant and fills in a likely explanation
for how Pierce and Rice knew where to find

00:03:34.750 --> 00:03:37.189
Laura and the other refugee mutant children.

00:03:37.189 --> 00:03:41.940
A deleted scene which didn't really affect
the final film sees Logan, Laura and Charles

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:46.550
pulled over by a police officer for speeding
after leaving the casino at Oklahoma City.

00:03:46.550 --> 00:03:50.920
For this moment, director James Mangold was
going for the feel of the road movie Little

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:55.080
Miss Sunshine where the family gets pulled
over by a cop on their journey to a beauty

00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:56.080
pageant.

00:03:56.080 --> 00:04:00.110
Although this scene is a little humorous and
includes Laura acknowledging Logan as her

00:04:00.110 --> 00:04:04.670
father, the scene was nearly two minutes long
and it's easy to see why it got cut.

00:04:04.670 --> 00:04:09.050
The Munson family dinner scene also had a
much darker alternative version.

00:04:09.050 --> 00:04:13.420
In the alternate, partly improvised, scene,
Kathryn asks Logan if he was ever married,

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:17.829
and Charles takes over the conversation saying
that Logan was married one time, except that

00:04:17.829 --> 00:04:20.340
now she's dead because Logan killed her.

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:24.560
This revelation by Charles is, obviously,
pretty awkward for Logan, and although Mangold

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:28.780
has said that he wanted the dinner scene to
provide some levity, this version actually

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:33.570
feels a bit too dark given everything that's
about to happen at the Munson family home.

00:04:33.570 --> 00:04:38.780
And although according to Mangold, Logan stands
alone as an X-Men film and he tried to avoid

00:04:38.780 --> 00:04:43.720
getting tied down connecting it to the wider
X-Men movie universe, this scene might still

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:49.310
have been confusing because the X-Men timeline
after Days of Future Past appeared to invalidate

00:04:49.310 --> 00:04:54.720
the first three X-Men movies including Wolverine's
killing of Jean Grey in The Last Stand.

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:58.880
So, what do you think about the deleted scenes
and where there any you think should have

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:00.070
been in the movie?

00:05:00.070 --> 00:05:03.790
And which X-Men movie or TV series are you
most looking forward to next?

